ition":"A chemical compound that can be excited by light of a specific wavelength and subsequently transfer energy to a chosen reactant. This is commonly molecular oxygen within a cancer tissue, which is converted to (highly rective) singlet state oxygen. This rapidly reacts with any nearby biomolecules, ultimately killing the cancer cells.","id":47868,"biological_role":false},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:132992","name":"radiosensitizing agent","definition":"A drug that makes increases the sensitivity of tumour cells to radiation therapy.","id":132992,"biological_role":false},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:59517","name":"DNA synthesis inhibitor","definition":"Any substance that inhibits the synthesis of DNA.","id":59517,"biological_role":true},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:36044","name":"antiviral drug","definition":"A substance used in the prophylaxis or therapy of virus diseases.","id":36044,"biological_role":true},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:74213","name":"EC 1.17.4.1 (ribonucleoside-diphosphate reductase) inhibitor","definition":"An  EC 1.17.* (oxidoreductase acting on CH or CH<small><sub>2</sub></small>) inhibitor that inhibits the action of ribonucleoside-diphosphate reductase (EC 1.17.4.1).","id":74213,"biological_role":true},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:35705","name":"immunosuppressive agent","definition":"An agent that suppresses immune function by one of several mechanisms of action. Classical cytotoxic immunosuppressants act by inhibiting DNA synthesis. Others may act through activation of T-cells or by inhibiting the activation of helper cells. In addition, an immunosuppressive agent is a role played by a compound which is exhibited by a capability to diminish the extent and/or voracity of an immune response.","id":35705,"biological_role":true},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:35221","name":"antimetabolite","definition":"A substance which is structurally similar to a metabolite but which competes with it or replaces it, and so prevents or reduces its normal utilization.","id":35221,"biological_role":true},{"application":false,"chemical_role":true,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:78298","name":"environmental contaminant","definition":"Any minor or unwanted substance introduced into the environment that can have undesired effects.","id":78298,"biological_role":false},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:35474","name":"anxiolytic drug","definition":"Anxiolytic drugs are agents that alleviate anxiety, tension, and anxiety disorders, promote sedation, and have a calming effect without affecting clarity of consciousness or neurologic conditions.","id":35474,"biological_role":false},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:35610","name":"antineoplastic agent","definition":"A substance that inhibits or prevents the proliferation of neoplasms.","id":35610,"biological_role":false},{"application":true,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:50266","name":"prodrug","definition":"A compound that, on administration, must undergo chemical conversion by metabolic processes before becoming the pharmacologically active drug for which it is a prodrug.","id":50266,"biological_role":false},{"application":false,"chemical_role":false,"chebi_accession":"CHEBI:35703","name":"xenobiotic","definition":"A xenobiotic (Greek, <em>xenos</em> \"foreign\"; <em>bios</em> \"life\") is a compound that is foreign to a living organism. Principal xenobiotics include: drugs, carcinogens and various compounds that have been introduced into the environment by artificial means.","id":35703,"biological_role":true}],"is_released":true}